WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:05.490
PETER BAVNICK: What
I'm going to talk

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.780
about is the standard
APIs, machine learning APIs

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.630
that we have available that you
can start using today to make

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.430
your applications smarter.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:19.200
So let's take one step back and
talk for a second about what

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.820
machine learning is.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.470
Machine learning is a
method of teaching computers

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:30.720
to recognize patterns based
on examples and experience.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.690
So it's very easy for
a child to understand

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.090
the difference between
the picture of a cat

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.010
and a picture of a dog.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.290
But for computers this is
actually very, very hard.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.775
So if you would start to
write rules to differentiate

00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:47.190
shape between a
cat and a dog, you

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.690
have to build a lot of rules--

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.980
create a set of rules.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.660
And pretty much for every set
that you can come up with,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.140
anyone can pull
out a picture that

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.960
is not going to match the
rules that you've thought of.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.050
So instead of writing
those rules yourself

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.000
you want the machine to learn
the rules by themselves,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.010
and actually learn
over time and have

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.450
them become smarter as well.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.940
And that's what
we've been doing,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.070
and we have trained different
APIs or different models

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.470
with a vast amount of data.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.240
And they're pre-trained
and you can use them.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.100
So as an example
with cats and dogs,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.280
this is a neural net that's one
of the mechanisms for machine

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.780
learning.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:37.050
It gets its inputs
from its raw pixels,

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.690
image points of the image.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.600
And with different
layers it learns

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.720
how to recognize
certain small pieces

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.950
in the picture such as
an edge, or a shape,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.910
or that maybe the fur
is a little bit fuzzy.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.880
So the higher you
get in the neural net

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.970
the higher level of abstraction
the neural net actually

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.410
extracts out of the image.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:06.870
And at the top layer there
are only two neurons left.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.139
We call them neurons because
this neural net is obviously

00:02:10.139 --> 00:02:12.900
modeled after our own brains.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.060
And the two neurons
basically specify,

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.190
is this picture of a dog,
or is this picture of a cat?

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.190
And in this case, it is a dog.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.490
So let's do a small
experiment and try

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:32.970
to illustrate the difficulty of
not having a machine learning

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.470
model learning the
rules, and let's

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.240
see if we can come up with
some rules of ourselves

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:41.340
of a human powered
image detection.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.660
So let's start.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.810
So let's say we want to write
some rules to differentiate

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.100
between apples and oranges.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.910
So what would be a
distinctive feature

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.950
that you could use to separate
the two types of image?

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.740
And you can shout out.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:56.240
Color.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:56.740
Color.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.460
PETER BAVNICK: Color Stem.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.470
Color, let's go with color.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.320
Apple, orange greenish,
orange, orange.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.420
Should work, right?

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.560
But what if the data that
you get is not only in color,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:11.730
it's black and white?

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.719
You immediately have a problem.

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:14.760
And this is what I meant.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.190
With any set that
you can bring, I

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.160
can give you a picture that's
probably not going to work.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.242
So how about if you
add another fruit?

00:03:23.242 --> 00:03:24.450
You have to start over again.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.100
Because now there's a new
shape that is being introduced.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.590
So the more different types
of data that you have,

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:38.760
the more complex your data set
or your rule set has to become.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.110
OK, but that's fruit, right?

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.420
I mean, fruit, they're
all very similar.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.950
That's actually quite difficult.
So let's make two things

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.760
that you would never think
of as these being the same.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.830
So let's say we want to
classify mops from dogs.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.900
Should be easy, right?

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.490
Well, maybe not so.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.510
So this is very
difficult. Actually I

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.010
had to look at some
of the pictures,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.620
and I'm sure some of you are
doing the same now like, well,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.280
what is this?

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.470
Is this-- what is this?

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:12.880
Is this a dog?

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.150
It is a dog, actually.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.640
So this is difficult, right?

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.980
But it even gets
more difficult. What

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:22.760
if you want to classify
all the images?

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.120
Everything, any object
you want to recognize it.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.590
Anybody using Google Photos?

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.540
Yeah.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.790
Google Photos is really,
really great at this.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.550
And it's powered by
the same Vision API

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.800
that we're going to
look at in a minute.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.650
So what if you not only
want to process images,

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:42.350
but you also want
to process video,

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.400
as we saw in the keynote?

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.250
Or you have audio, or you
have unstructured text

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:51.300
that you want to extract
information from?

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.690
So that's the space, and
that's the reason why

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.940
machine learning is
becoming so popular,

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:58.130
because it can handle
some really, really

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:00.000
complex problems.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.290
So with Google we have
basically two solutions

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.620
or offerings that can help
you with machine learning.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.670
So on the left hand side
you will see our open source

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:15.830
library that is, let's say, six
years of work that we've open

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:16.400
sourced now.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.360
It's called TensorFlow.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:22.250
It's the most popular machine
learning library on GitHub.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.560
So if you haven't
looked at it, I strongly

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.820
suggest you have a look at it.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.950
It's very, very powerful.

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:31.340
It's running many of the
machine learning stuff

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:32.960
that we're doing.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.090
And you have Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:38.150
So in TensorFlow you
can define your model

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.570
and you can specify how
to train your model,

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:45.020
but now you need the compute
and the storage resources

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.240
where you actually train it.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.990
And believe me, training these
models takes a lot of storage,

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:52.990
and it takes a lot
of compute power.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:54.470
A lot of computer power.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.710
So probably the best way
to do it is in the cloud.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:01.220
And obviously we think the best
place to do it is in our cloud.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:05.030
And Cloud Machine
Learning Engine does that.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:10.290
It leverages our CPUs, GPUs,
and the custom built TPUs

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.180
that we've announced also.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.430
So on the right hand side you
don't have to do any of that.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.740
We've done it for you.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.040
We have provided the data,
we have trained these models,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.230
and all you need to do
is provide your sample

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:30.380
and we do basically
the classification

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.030
and tell you what's
in the image.

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.750
So these are pre-trained
out of the box APIs

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.180
that you can leverage
in your applications

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.480
with a single REST goal.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.740
All right, let's start
with the Vision API.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.280
So the Vision API is an API it
does complex image recognition

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.170
through a single call.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:53.660
And one example was
already briefly mentioned

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.280
in the keynote is realtor.com.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.270
It's a real estate
agent, and it's

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.820
using the Vision API in
their mobile application.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.660
So when you're
looking for houses

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:09.380
and you see a house for sale,
you can pick up your phone,

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:12.080
take a picture, or you
could point your camera

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.630
at the sign that's there.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.370
It will extract the
information and it

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:18.590
will pull up all the
information of the house

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:20.930
from the realtor.com website.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.690
So it's just very
easy for someone just

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.050
to find the listing
on their website.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.820
So what are some of
the features that are

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.230
available in the Vision API?

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.520
So the first most known
feature is label detection.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.520
So basically the Vision API will
tell you what's in the picture.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.970
Is it a dog, is it a cat, right?

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.900
The second thing
is face detection.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.600
So it will recognize
that there are

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.000
multiple faces in the images.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.000
It will exactly point out
where those images are.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.130
And even further than
that, it will give you

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:56.970
some impression if this person
is angry, happy, and so forth.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.710
The third one, OCR, optical
character recognition.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:05.130
So basically it can understand
what text is in the image.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.580
So it will read the text
and it will output it

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.000
in text so you can actually
[INAUDIBLE] it much easier.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.220
It will also detect
explicit content

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.444
like nudity, violence,
blood, and it

00:08:15.444 --> 00:08:16.610
will give you an indication.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:19.460
This is a great feature
if you, for instance,

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.790
in your application you provide
your end users to upload images

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.970
but you want to prevent that
inappropriate images are

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.770
being uploaded.

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.390
Then landmark detection.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:35.270
Both natural landmarks as well
as human created landmarks

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:38.996
are detected, and we're going to
show you what that looks like.

00:08:38.996 --> 00:08:40.620
And last but not
least, logo detection.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.690
So if you are worried
about copyrights,

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:49.310
then we can actually
detect logos in the image

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.430
and flag that for you.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:54.560
So how difficult is it?

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.650
Well, I already mentioned
it, a single call.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:00.680
So if you look at this
Node.js code, it's--

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.880
you have to include the Google
Cloud library, of course.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:07.780
You set up a configuration
JSON structure,

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.420
and basically there's one call.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:13.690
You provide the data,
you specify a callback,

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:17.440
because you need to handle
the response that comes back,

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:18.620
and that's it.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.870
It's that simple.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.350
So here's a picture I took
last year when I was being

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:25.080
a total tourist in New York.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.830
It was actually my
first time in New York,

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:30.130
so I spent a day
just walking around.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:33.370
And of course I had to visit
the Statue of Liberty, right?

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:34.510
And I took this picture.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:38.170
And you can do this yourself.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.930
These APIs have
demos on our website.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.970
So I just uploaded this
picture on our website

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:45.330
and this is one that came back.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.620
So it recognized the Statue
of Liberty correctly.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:51.280
It gave it 55%
confidence rating,

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.670
and that's probably because
I'm on the back side

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:56.812
of the Statue of Liberty.

00:09:56.812 --> 00:09:59.020
So it was probably missing
some distinctive features,

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.060
like the face and the crown.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.820
But still it detected it,
it provided the location

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:04.930
where it is.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.570
So that's landmark detection.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:10.180
This is what you get
back from the API.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.310
So now it's in
programming language,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:18.362
it's an JSON structure, and
you can process it accordingly.

00:10:18.362 --> 00:10:19.570
So here's the face detection.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.360
And I would like to ask
your special attention

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.260
for the expressionlessness
of my face in this picture.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.469
Joy is very unlikely,
sorrow is very unlikely,

00:10:33.469 --> 00:10:35.260
anger is very-- so I
think I should pick up

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.250
poker or something like that.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.060
But you will notice
it will accurately

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:41.530
provide where are my eyes?

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:42.580
Where are my eyebrows?

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:43.490
Where's my chin?

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:46.390
It even gives me suggestions
on where to crop.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.274
If I want to use this, for
instance, as a profile picture,

00:10:49.274 --> 00:10:51.440
it gives you the dimensions,
OK, you should probably

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.070
crop it right there.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:56.140
That's face detection.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:00.107
Then label detections is if
I push the right button--

00:11:00.107 --> 00:11:01.690
by the way, this is
what you get back.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.850
It's the same information,
but then, of course,

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.010
in a JSON structure.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.900
Then label detection.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:13.720
And I'm actually very impressed
with the label detection.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:16.230
If you look at what it gets
from it, well, it gets a person.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.940
Well, duh, I see that, right?

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:21.415
But it also noticed
people in the background.

00:11:21.415 --> 00:11:23.640
That's also pretty good.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:24.520
But look at this.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:28.120
It thinks this is a vacation
picture, which it was.

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:29.590
Why does it do that?

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:30.460
I'm not really sure.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:33.110
Probably the setting.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:34.750
That it was the
Statue of Liberty,

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:37.120
and it's likely that
it was a vacation.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.440
That there are people there,
maybe the weather was nice.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:41.230
And look at this.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.960
It picked up travel, probably
because I have a backpack.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.649
It recognized I have a
strap around my shoulder.

00:11:46.649 --> 00:11:48.940
So this type of information
that you get from the label

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.240
detection.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:53.001
And this is what you get back
in JSON, and with the confidence

00:11:53.001 --> 00:11:53.500
rating.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.070
And you can act
upon it as you wish.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:58.990
All right, so I
mentioned crop hints.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.795
A very interesting feature
is web annotations,

00:12:01.795 --> 00:12:03.784
and I'm going to show
you an example of that.

00:12:03.784 --> 00:12:05.200
And another thing
that we recently

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.790
announced an extension of,
or an additional feature

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:12.070
in the Vision API is a
document text annotations.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.110
And what that
actually does, it is

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:17.430
able to extract large
amounts of text.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:20.170
So it's really powerful,
for instance, scanning.

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:22.330
Scans documents, or
if you take a picture

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:24.880
of a document or
a large sign, it

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.080
will process that
text correctly.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:30.132
So web annotations.

00:12:30.132 --> 00:12:31.090
What's web annotations?

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.100
So basically web
annotations provides you

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.820
additional information on
where this image resides

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:39.860
on the internet, who
else is using it,

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.420
and that sort of stuff.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:45.100
So this car, who
recognizes this car?

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.725
Yeah, what is it?

00:12:47.725 --> 00:12:51.824
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:51.824 --> 00:12:52.990
PETER BAVNICK: That's great.

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.270
That's the car used in
the Harry Potter movies.

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:58.450
So it recognizes
what the car is.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:00.340
But surprisingly
it also tells you

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:03.720
that it's in the Art
Science Museum in Singapore,

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.100
and it will also provide
that it recognizes this

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.780
is from the Harry Potter movie.

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:13.800
You will also notice that you
will find these types of links

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.090
here there, and these are
references to the knowledge

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:16.590
graphs.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:20.890
So you can add an additional
API call to the Google Knowledge

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:23.200
Graph to even extract
more information

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.550
if you wish to do so.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.150
So then it finds, OK, where
did this image come from?

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:32.800
What are other places where
this image is currently stored?

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:36.220
And perhaps there might
be variants of that image.

00:13:36.220 --> 00:13:38.260
Maybe someone flipped
it horizontally,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:42.910
or maybe someone cropped
it, it will still detect it.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.950
All right, like
I said, all these

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:48.030
APIs you can try
out yourself for fun

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:49.930
on our website,
cloud.google.com,

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:54.370
and then for the different APIs
go to Vision, Speech, Natural

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.710
Language and so forth.

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:58.210
All right, so if
you're wondering

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.900
how well did Vision API with
those dogs and mops pictures,

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:04.870
I think it did
actually pretty well.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:09.610
So it classified this one as
not only a dog and a mammal,

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:12.190
but it also noticed that
it's a komondor breed.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.980
So it goes even further
than just saying it's a dog.

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:17.350
And for the rest--

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.060
this one also correct.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.130
And for the rest it almost
got every one right,

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.930
so I don't know if this
one is a hit or a miss.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.230
It's fur.

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:26.200
Yeah, it's fur.

00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:29.940
But it doesn't know if it's
a dog or any other animal.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:34.570
And textile, let's
say it's not correct.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.449
So pretty good.

00:14:36.449 --> 00:14:38.490
All right, so the second
API I want to talk about

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:39.550
is the Speech API.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.037
So to Speech API
basically writes

00:14:42.037 --> 00:14:43.120
what you're saying, right?

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:45.400
It's the technology
that's behind Google Home,

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.960
it's behind voice
search on your phone,

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:51.140
and you can just include it
in your applications as well.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:54.370
And we do this transcription
in over 80 languages.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.620
So a company that is
using that is Azar.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:15:01.180
Azar connects currently
almost 20 billion people,

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:04.270
or has made almost 20 billion
connections between people

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:05.470
around the globe.

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.050
And they use the Speech
API in a combination

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.690
with the translation
API so that people

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.690
in different regions speaking
different languages can hook up

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:17.140
and can easily communicate.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:18.910
So let's see a demo.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:23.680
So this demo is
using the following.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.170
It's using a command
line library called

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:28.930
Socks for the audio recording.

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.240
It's using a bash script,
so it's one way to do it.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.120
We're going to see
some different demos.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:37.870
This is a bash script
simply calling a REST API

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.760
through a cURL command.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.130
So we set up a
JSON configuration

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:45.130
and we'll send the request, and
we'll see what it looks like.

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:48.940
Let's switch to the demo please.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.900
So talking to your
machine at a live stage

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:52.960
is always interesting.

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:56.810
So as I said, it's using
different languages.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:59.710
So I'm going to speak in Dutch.

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:05.290
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:08.320
All right, so this is the
JSON that we've prepared.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.320
You have to provide some
additional information

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.970
to the Speech API, like
what is the format?

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:13.720
It's FLAC.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.940
What's the sample
rate being used?

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.220
You don't have to
specify the language.

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:19.600
Then it will default to English.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.610
But in this case,
I'm providing NONL,

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:24.010
which is the locale
for Dutch, and I'm

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:27.830
going to talk about the
phrases context in a minute.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:32.580
So let's see what the Speech
API actually comes back with.

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.960
That's always
interesting for me.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:37.245
All right, so this
is actually quite--

00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:39.820
this is good because this
helps me with my demo.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:46.570
So it says, [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]
So it almost got everything

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:47.230
right.

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:50.440
And that's why we have
that phrases context.

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:53.080
Because you might be
working with product names

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:55.970
that might not be very
familiar in the language.

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:57.500
It might be a specific name.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:00.640
And in this case, the
word API, probably

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.890
in Dutch we might
say api, right?

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:07.190
So let's head over to the code.

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:09.339
So this is the code that
is running that script,

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:10.420
and I can give hints.

00:17:10.420 --> 00:17:17.319
So let's add API as a
keyword to the Speech API.

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:19.810
So when we run it,
it will actually--

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:23.109
hopefully-- better
recognize what I'm saying.

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:24.849
All right, let's try this again.

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:31.030
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:35.250
You will notice now that the
[DUTCH] has the API keyword

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:40.730
in there, and yay, success.

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:44.420
So now it correctly identifies
API as being a word.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:47.510
So that can be used in, like
I said, for product names,

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:50.210
or maybe specific
words that you want

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:53.180
to look for in the Speech API.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:57.270
All right, switch back
to the slides please.

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.920
So how difficult is it
to call the Speech API?

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.630
Well, you will see the
same paradigm every time.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:05.370
The same lines of code.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:08.010
You include the libraries,
set up the configuration,

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.140
do the call, specify a
callback, and that's it.

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:15.990
All right.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.840
So the third API that
I want to talk about

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.440
is the Natural Language API.

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:22.680
So the Natural
Language API, so where

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.760
speech translates
it to text, now you

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.510
want to do something
with the text, right?

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:31.230
You want to see what
is the text about?

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:33.270
Is it positive, is it negative?

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:36.780
What information can I
extract from these entities

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:37.590
in the text?

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:42.180
And that's exactly what the
Natural Language API does.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:43.970
So one of our
customers is Wootric,

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.020
who is using the
Natural Language API.

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:50.100
They provide a customer
satisfaction or a customer

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:53.070
survey platform,
and their customers

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:59.760
use Wootric's services to
solicit for customer feedback.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:01.390
And they do this
in different way.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:05.220
So they have a typical
scoring from 0 to 10, which

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:10.170
is very easy to get a sense
of, is it positive or negative?

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:13.009
But they also provide
a free text field.

00:19:13.009 --> 00:19:14.300
And that's a lot harder, right?

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:16.010
So what is it about?

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:19.470
OK, you say it's
eight but is that--

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:22.800
what were you so
satisfied about?

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.590
So that's a lot more difficult.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:28.680
They also use it to basically
do a consistency check.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:33.810
So if you say a 3,
but in the free text

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:38.070
you're actually quite positive,
they can figure it out.

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:42.340
And they then can act upon that.

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:46.290
Also obviously, if you
get negative feedback

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:47.640
you want to take action, right?

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:50.310
So they also provide
near real time

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:53.340
routing off customer
feedback onto,

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:56.490
let's say, a contact center
where they can follow up.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.190
They call the customer to say,
hey, we saw this feedback,

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:01.770
we want to talk to you.

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:04.410
All right, so I've taken
this from the Wikipedia.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:06.750
I'm going to stay with
the Harry Potter theme.

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.240
I took this from
the Wikipedia page,

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:11.510
and let's see what
happens when we throw it

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:13.410
at the Natural Language API.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:17.670
So what's interesting is it
pools Joanne 'Jo' Rowling,

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:20.820
JK Rowling, and also
Robert Galbraith, who

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:25.110
is another pen name for her
for another book series.

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:28.230
And these actually come
back as the same entity.

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.470
So it knows this.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.790
And it will know it's a person.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:39.750
Here again, that m-id, which is
a link to the Google Knowledge

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:43.170
Graph, where additional
information can be found.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:48.330
British, it clearly identified
it as a location with a link

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:49.970
to the United Kingdom.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:55.660
And obviously, it found Harry
Potter as an entity, as well.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.810
So the second thing that
the Natural Language API

00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:02.040
can do-- so not only extract the
entities, so what it is about,

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:06.120
but it can also detect is it
positive, or is it negative?

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:09.180
And it does so by
providing a scoring.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:12.450
And the scoring goes
from minus 1 to plus 1,

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:14.790
indicating whether it's
positive or negative.

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.470
But it will also
provide a magnitude.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.670
And a magnitude basically
is an indication

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:22.520
which goes from 0 to
infinity, is an indication.

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:28.350
So how many text was used to
being positive or negative?

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:33.330
So the more text you provide
negatively the number goes up.

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:38.850
And that's also very
valuable to process.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.130
So the third thing the
Natural Language API does--

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:45.990
and maybe this is somewhat
helpful for your application

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:46.740
as well--

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.480
is it analyzes the syntax.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:51.960
So the first thing it does
is it basically creates

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.700
a dependency parse tree.

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.500
So it understand
what the relationship

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.590
is between the words and how
they relate to each other.

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:04.800
It will provide information
on what the role of the word

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:05.770
is in the sentence.

00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:09.510
So is it the root verb,
is it the nominal subject?

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.360
That sort of information.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:16.890
It provides lemma, which is
done for the word help here.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:21.840
And basically it gives back
the canonical form of the word.

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:26.670
So it will not take into account
different version of the word.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:30.150
So you might have
helped, helping, help.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:31.845
If you were counting
for certain words,

00:22:31.845 --> 00:22:33.720
for instance, you don't
want to differentiate

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:35.160
between these versions.

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:38.280
You want the canonical form
you want to use and count help.

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.905
And we're going to see
that in the demo as well.

00:22:40.905 --> 00:22:42.420
And last but not
least, it provides

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:43.380
additional information.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.380
Was it plural, was it singular?

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:48.120
So basically
morphology information.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.610
All right, so let's see a demo.

00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:55.900
This demo I created
in the following way.

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:59.070
So basically what it's doing
is I've subscribed to Twitter.

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:04.360
I'm looking for tweets around
machine learning, big data,

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:08.250
artificial intelligence,
those sort of keywords.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.800
I'm passing every
tweet that I get back,

00:23:10.800 --> 00:23:13.940
I pass it into the
Natural Language API

00:23:13.940 --> 00:23:17.180
to see if it's positive,
if it's negative.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:19.260
I store this in two
Google solutions.

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:21.874
So one is into
Firebase database,

00:23:21.874 --> 00:23:23.290
which is a real
time database that

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:26.930
can be basically I use
that to get notifications.

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:29.150
Whenever it's changed
in the database

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:33.350
I get notified in a browser so
it's very easy to see updates.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:35.780
And I use BigQuery, where
I store all the tweets.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:38.000
So the Firebase database
only basically keeps

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:40.970
track of the last one,
and the BigQuery database

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:43.122
stores all the tweets
that I pulled up.

00:23:43.122 --> 00:23:44.330
If we can switch to the demo.

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:53.080
So here we see the front end.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:56.920
So basically the tweets
are coming in live.

00:23:56.920 --> 00:24:00.040
On the big blue horizontal
bar, you see the sentiment

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:03.880
that it detects, whether
it's positive or negative.

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:09.430
And so I ran this yesterday,
and then the numbers came back.

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:12.170
And I'm looking for
adjectives, by the way.

00:24:12.170 --> 00:24:16.690
And surprise surprise, if
you look for deep learning,

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:19.180
big data, artificial
intelligence,

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:23.920
guess what the most popular
adjectives are going to be?

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:26.320
Deep, big, and artificial.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.330
So well, it's working.

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:30.340
But it's not
senseless data, right?

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:34.270
So you will see that
the Natural Language

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:37.930
API was able to get the tweet,
understand the sentence,

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.750
extract the
adjectives, the nouns,

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:45.120
count the number of adjectives,
and put out this information.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.870
I'm not showing the
tweets, because what

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:51.070
can go wrong showing
live tweets on the stage?

00:24:51.070 --> 00:24:54.790
Here you see-- which is I
guess more interesting--

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:57.640
the scoring, whether
it's positive.

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:01.870
So generally speaking
tweets are relatively

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:05.710
positive about these things.

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:09.696
So that's the
Firebase, or that's

00:25:09.696 --> 00:25:11.320
the front end on the
Firebase database.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:13.600
Let's switch to BigQuery.

00:25:13.600 --> 00:25:18.090
So BigQuery is our large
scale data warehouse solution.

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:23.175
And so here I store
the tweets, and I'm

00:25:23.175 --> 00:25:24.550
going to scroll
to the right just

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:28.390
to prevent strange
tweets being in there.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:30.580
And so I store the
tweets, I store

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:32.840
some additional information--
the user, time zone,

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:36.890
number of follow, of course,
the hashtags, very important.

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:40.960
But here you will notice
this huge JSON string

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:45.940
which is the output I get back
from the Natural Language API.

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:49.720
So looking at this
some of you might

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:54.640
wonder if I want to query this,
and we heard in the keynote

00:25:54.640 --> 00:25:58.390
that you can use
standard SQL to query,

00:25:58.390 --> 00:26:01.540
how the heck am I going
to query this JSON string

00:26:01.540 --> 00:26:05.590
and look for these words?

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:12.630
Well, BigQuery
supports a mechanism--

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:16.860
and let me open the query
for you, oh, sorry--

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:27.480
where I can provide a
user defined function.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.570
So you can write
your own functions

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:32.360
and include those
functions in your queries,

00:26:32.360 --> 00:26:36.050
and actually we provide a
simple editor in the top

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:40.240
here where you can
see that I specify

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:44.150
the interface of the function,
basically what it expects,

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:46.920
and a write function as well.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.250
So basically what
it does, it takes

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:51.620
that JSON, it looks
for adjectives,

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:55.590
pulls out the information and
returns it as a structure.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:02.000
So now if I run this query
you will notice basically

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:03.000
the same results, right?

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:07.810
So I can query this information
even if it's stored as a JSON.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.750
And the great thing is
the power of BigQuery

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:12.430
comes from parallelism.

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:16.510
So it distributes the query
over thousands of nodes,

00:27:16.510 --> 00:27:18.629
and each node does
the simple thing.

00:27:18.629 --> 00:27:20.170
And even these user
defined functions

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:22.150
are distributed
across those nodes

00:27:22.150 --> 00:27:24.201
to perform very well, as well.

00:27:24.201 --> 00:27:26.200
All right, if you can
switch back to the slides.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:31.710
So this is how you call
the Natural Language API.

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:33.510
Surprise, surprise.

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:37.050
Same thing, set up to
[INAUDIBLE] provide the file,

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:39.960
set up a callback,
and that's it.

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:42.705
So one of the newer
features we've released,

00:27:42.705 --> 00:27:45.660
and I hope you see
this now that even

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:49.920
after we released these
APIs we keep adding features

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:50.730
to these APIs.

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:53.640
So we saw the new features
of the Vision API,

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:57.150
here you see a new feature
of the Natural Language API.

00:27:57.150 --> 00:28:00.090
And this new feature is
called entity based sentiment.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:05.070
So instead of providing a
sentiment over the whole text,

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:08.580
we extract the entities
and we provide information

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:11.490
what the sentiment is
of that specific entity

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.150
in the sentence.

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:14.470
So that's more powerful.

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:15.870
Let's look at this example.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:19.570
I really like the sushi but
the service was terrible.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:22.980
So I'm positive on one side but
I'm negative on the other side.

00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:25.800
So what am I positive about
and what am I negative about?

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:29.420
And that is provided here as
an example, well, positive,

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:36.270
0.18 is the score, and negative
for the service, a minus 0.9.

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.660
All right, look at
Translation API.

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:42.015
Translation API, or the
Google Translation service

00:28:42.015 --> 00:28:47.760
is one of those very familiar,
widely used functionalities.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:51.910
It supports the most
languages, 100 plus languages.

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:55.290
And you can use it in your
application out of the box.

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:59.910
So everyone knows Airbnb,
one of the customers

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:02.910
that is using the
translation API.

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:08.820
Basically 60% of their bookings
is done in a different language

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:11.780
than the posting was in.

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:13.620
And they know that
if you provide

00:29:13.620 --> 00:29:16.950
the comments, the
conversations, the listing,

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:19.080
the additional information,
if you provide that

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.960
in a native language,
then the chance of booking

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.200
is much higher.

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:25.920
So the translation
API is actually

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:30.150
a very important component
of their offering.

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:31.800
So this is how you call it.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.110
Same thing.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:38.040
And looking at the neural
machine translation,

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:39.670
which is the newer version--

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:41.910
so basically last year
we released a new version

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:42.975
of the translation API.

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:48.540
And despite the great work
that the translation team had

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:53.190
done over the last eight or
nine years it was for them

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:58.440
I guess maybe sad to see
that by injection of machine

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:01.830
learning into the
translation service

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:07.260
it actually gained a 40%
improvement over the quality.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:09.810
So imagine, we've
been optimizing,

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:12.270
and Google Translate was
already pretty awesome

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:14.400
if you compare it
with other services.

00:30:14.400 --> 00:30:17.940
But then if you already
spent so much time and effort

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:22.080
in getting it so at that
level, and then with machine

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:27.990
learning you get a 40% bump
in the quality, that is huge.

00:30:27.990 --> 00:30:29.130
That is huge.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.210
So that's the power
of machine learning.

00:30:31.210 --> 00:30:34.317
So the old way it
worked, we didn't

00:30:34.317 --> 00:30:35.400
look at the whole context.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:40.050
So basically we did a word for
word translation of the words.

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:42.059
And we did recognize
that there's

00:30:42.059 --> 00:30:43.100
some relationship, right?

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:45.000
So if it's singular
and plural, we

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.220
will adjust the verbs to that.

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:50.070
But it was basically that.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:53.070
And with machine
learning translation,

00:30:53.070 --> 00:30:55.540
we actually take a
different approach.

00:30:55.540 --> 00:31:00.780
We've trained a model with
sentences and simple sentences

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:04.800
to illustrate the difference
between the quality--

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.730
I've taken this from the
original Spanish text of one

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:10.440
of the Harry Potter books.

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:13.260
So when I say original,
I mean human translated.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:17.190
The book was an English, it got
translated by human in Spanish.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.420
That's what I took.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:21.750
And this is the difference
between the two translation

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:22.750
services.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.130
So the first
generation translation,

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:27.990
it said things like, a
company that made drills.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:31.120
Well if it's making drills,
then it's a manufacturer.

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:34.290
So the proper way to say that
would be it manufactured it.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:37.260
And neckless, I don't even
know if that's actually a word,

00:31:37.260 --> 00:31:38.400
neckless.

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:40.170
So without a neck.

00:31:40.170 --> 00:31:42.810
And almost twice
longer than usual.

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:45.870
That's kind of a weird way of
saying that in English, right?

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:49.530
And we've all played around
with Google translate, I assume.

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.540
And you understand
what it's about,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.600
but you kind of go,
huh, I wouldn't send it

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:57.720
to my customer like that.

00:31:57.720 --> 00:32:01.950
If you have that feeling, have a
look at Google Translate again.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:04.830
Because it is significantly
better than it

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:07.530
was before powered by machinery.

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:10.890
And luckily for you,
this API is available

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:14.880
and it benefits from the
same improvement, as well.

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:21.120
All right, so with this demo I'm
going to do a couple of things.

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:22.860
So I'm not going to
do the speech again,

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:25.590
but I'm going to do the Vision.

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:27.950
So I'm combining APIs now.

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:32.090
Because I think that's
the power of these APIs.

00:32:32.090 --> 00:32:36.040
So I'm going to provide some
text just as an example,

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:38.150
take it to the
Natural Language API,

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:41.000
then I'm going to send
it to the translation API

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:42.440
and see what the results are.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:44.450
And I'm going to do
the same with an image.

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:46.285
And the image is of a resume.

00:32:46.285 --> 00:32:47.910
So if we can switch
to the demo please.

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:57.160
All right, so I've
started up the demo.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:59.730
So let's start with some text.

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:12.410
All right, it's going to
extract some information.

00:33:12.410 --> 00:33:17.030
So I'm not going to output the
whole JSON because that will

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:21.320
fill the pages of the screen.

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:24.080
So I only took the first word,
the first entity it found,

00:33:24.080 --> 00:33:24.950
this.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:29.660
It says it's the nominal
subject of the sentence

00:33:29.660 --> 00:33:31.410
and it provides information.

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.510
And for a lot of things,
this, it doesn't provide.

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:36.120
There's no gender in this,
and that sort of stuff.

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:38.330
But you will get
that information

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:41.670
for all the entities
in the word.

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:43.160
So we did a sentiment analysis.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:49.040
Says, you are pretty awesome--
sorry, you're pretty neutral.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:50.930
I was supposed to say
awesome in the sentence

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:52.970
and then it's going to
say that it was great.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:54.210
So this is pretty neutral.

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:55.260
That's fine.

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:58.550
And the entities
found, it found demo

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.520
and it found Translation API.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:07.790
Now let's send it to
the Translation API.

00:34:07.790 --> 00:34:09.920
And I don't know if
anyone knows Spanish,

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:13.429
but hopefully that is a
pretty accurate translation.

00:34:13.429 --> 00:34:17.420
Just in case, I translated the
translation back to English

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:20.570
so you can see what
it came back as.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:24.929
So let's do this again,
but now provide an image.

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:29.949
And the image that
I have is this.

00:34:29.949 --> 00:34:34.429
So this is an example of
the large amount of text

00:34:34.429 --> 00:34:35.389
that is in the image.

00:34:35.389 --> 00:34:38.159
That's one of the new
features of the Vision API.

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:40.340
So this is a resume,
it's just a sample resume

00:34:40.340 --> 00:34:42.199
I pulled off the internet.

00:34:42.199 --> 00:34:44.300
It has a lot of text in there.

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:48.050
Let's see if the Vision
API can actually grab that.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:50.719
So I'm telling it I'm
providing a picture.

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:58.930
It senses through the Vision
API and it attracts a lot

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:00.280
of information, as you can see.

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:06.970
Let's see if it
finds any entities,

00:35:06.970 --> 00:35:09.310
and it actually does, of course.

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:11.420
So there's a lot of
entities in there.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:15.460
So for instance, Jane worked
at the 3M incorporated,

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:19.810
so I get a link to additional
information for that entity

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:21.230
as well.

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:23.560
And you'll see a lot
of different entities

00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:29.570
that are found in the
text that's translated.

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:36.870
So here's the
resume in Japanese.

00:35:36.870 --> 00:35:39.600
And as I assume none of
you speak Japanese-- well,

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:41.580
maybe someone does--

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:43.260
it comes back to English.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:48.420
And you could see it it works
actually quite beautifully.

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:51.260
So this is an example of
combining different APIs.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:54.330
So the Vision API, take
it to the Natural Language

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:58.020
API to extract the entities,
taking it to the Translation

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:00.340
API to translate the document.

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:03.180
So you have meta data from
the Natural Language API,

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:06.480
you have to translate the
text so everyone can read it

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:07.684
and so forth.

00:36:07.684 --> 00:36:08.850
Let's go back to the slides.

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:16.350
All right, so you saw the
Video Intelligence API briefly

00:36:16.350 --> 00:36:20.010
in the keynote and I'm
going to spend a little bit

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:23.100
more time about it.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:25.380
So what it does, the
Video Intelligence API

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:28.420
is our newest m-l API.

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:32.010
So expect this to be
improved and enhanced

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:34.050
over the coming time.

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.667
What it does, it provides
information of the video

00:36:36.667 --> 00:36:37.500
at different levels.

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:41.430
So it provides it at video
level, at scene level,

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:44.970
and even at individual
frame level.

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:47.490
One of the companies that
is using that Cantemo.

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:50.310
Cantemo is a company
that provides

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:53.460
services around digital
asset management.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:57.270
And they love the
Video Intelligence API

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:59.750
because they provide
to their customers

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:02.700
a service where the
customers can actually

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:06.450
search their extensive
library of assets.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.570
So this is a quote from
the VP product development,

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:11.760
thanks to the Google Cloud
Video Intelligence API

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:14.550
we have been able to very
quickly process and understand

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:17.370
the content of video down
to the individual frame

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:19.750
with an impressively
rich taxonomy.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:22.600
So let's take a look
at what it can do.

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:26.320
So obviously the responses that
you get back in JSON format.

00:37:26.320 --> 00:37:28.130
So here's an example.

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:31.710
It's not telling us here
what is in the picture.

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:35.460
It's giving us information
about the framing.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:39.180
How was this frame,
this scene shot?

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:41.250
And this is a bird's eye view.

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:46.770
And as another example here,
it will recognize, look,

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:49.110
this is a portrait shot.

00:37:49.110 --> 00:37:54.430
So imagine that you are a
video company and you're--

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:56.070
have to make video,
you can simply

00:37:56.070 --> 00:38:00.150
search, OK, give me a
portrait shot of something,

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:04.200
a monkey in the forest
or something like that.

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:07.200
And you will find
in the vast library

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:10.380
of videos you will immediately
find the correct video.

00:38:10.380 --> 00:38:13.310
I think that's awesome.

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:14.810
All right, let's
switch to the demo.

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:26.350
So here I have our demo for
the Video Intelligence API--

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:28.880
let me make it a
little bit bigger.

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:33.586
And so I have different
videos, and I can--

00:38:33.586 --> 00:38:39.960
let's see, there was one that
I thought was funny, this one.

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:43.810
A tour of Google, and
let's play it for you.

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:48.010
So the video starts, there
are a couple of frames there.

00:38:48.010 --> 00:38:51.180
Pictures going by.

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:54.580
And then it starts
as a typical video.

00:38:54.580 --> 00:38:59.350
There are some
logos, some signs,

00:38:59.350 --> 00:39:02.050
and I'm going to
stop the video here.

00:39:02.050 --> 00:39:06.010
So you will notice here that
this bi-- and I actually, I

00:39:06.010 --> 00:39:08.590
didn't see this at first, and
it's funny how your eyes work,

00:39:08.590 --> 00:39:09.610
right?

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:14.410
Because I clicked on one of
the tags, which was bicycle,

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:16.840
and I had completely
missed the fact that there

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:19.030
are bicycles in this frame.

00:39:19.030 --> 00:39:21.040
Because I was
actually my eyes were

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:24.050
drawn to this person
walking towards me.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:26.860
And I didn't see the bicycles.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:29.560
But the fact that
my brain starts

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:31.150
to focus on certain
things, that's

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:33.280
something the computer
is not bothered by.

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:35.380
It will just analyze the frame.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:38.870
And so here you see
all kinds of tags.

00:39:38.870 --> 00:39:41.410
So there's a
miracle right there.

00:39:41.410 --> 00:39:43.090
We have statues.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:48.340
It's found that there's
fruit, which is correct.

00:39:48.340 --> 00:39:53.470
And I think ears here's
the bicycle, and that's--

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.930
if I can see it.

00:39:55.930 --> 00:39:59.860
Yeah, so it takes me,
indeed, to this shot.

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:02.270
So I think that's
very, very powerful.

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:07.510
So what we can do is we
can also do a search.

00:40:07.510 --> 00:40:09.880
Because we extracted
that metadata,

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:13.630
we stored it somewhere,
now we can query it.

00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:19.000
So we saw the
example of beaches.

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:23.020
And I make the same mistake
because we have a simple query

00:40:23.020 --> 00:40:24.160
there.

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:27.160
So here are the scenes
with beach in there.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:34.480
And if you're making a
video you can decide,

00:40:34.480 --> 00:40:38.290
do you want the first picture
or the second picture?

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:42.694
Different versions,
different shots,

00:40:42.694 --> 00:40:43.735
all kinds of information.

00:40:43.735 --> 00:40:46.465
Now let's do another
example, let's say baseball.

00:40:49.000 --> 00:40:52.660
So when you're searching
for some videos,

00:40:52.660 --> 00:40:56.060
then immediately from the
first line you will recognize,

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:58.900
oh, almost this entire video
is about baseball, right?

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:01.870
Because the whole line
is pretty much red.

00:41:01.870 --> 00:41:07.210
As opposed to the last video
where only a small tidbit

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:09.100
is about baseball.

00:41:09.100 --> 00:41:13.600
And if we open that
it correctly found it.

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:15.520
So I think this is
a very powerful way

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:18.010
to start working with video.

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:20.630
And to be honest,
this has been--

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:23.860
in machine learning,
processing video

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:26.920
is one of the hardest
nuts to crack.

00:41:26.920 --> 00:41:27.670
Why?

00:41:27.670 --> 00:41:30.340
Because if you look at
an image, it's static.

00:41:30.340 --> 00:41:32.180
You look at the imaging
and you process it.

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:35.590
But when you want
to process video,

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:39.030
you actually start looking
into time difference--

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:40.720
sorry, the time
dimension, as well,

00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:43.450
so you have to take into
account what is going on.

00:41:43.450 --> 00:41:46.720
So we go further than
just individual frames

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:49.150
where we see someone
with a baseball,

00:41:49.150 --> 00:41:53.180
we actually see the
movement as well.

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:56.210
All right, if we go
back to the slides.

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:57.970
So how did we build this demo?

00:41:57.970 --> 00:42:01.490
So we're using a couple of
Google Cloud Services here.

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:06.970
So we store all the videos
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:10.600
That's our massive storage
layer for basically binary

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:12.950
large objects.

00:42:12.950 --> 00:42:14.980
So we store it there,
and the great thing

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:17.440
about Cloud Storage
and Cloud Functions

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:20.630
is that you can subscribe
to changes in your bucket.

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:22.340
So if something
changes, like a video

00:42:22.340 --> 00:42:25.270
is uploaded to your
bucket, it actually

00:42:25.270 --> 00:42:28.210
notifies Cloud Functions,
and Cloud Functions

00:42:28.210 --> 00:42:34.990
is our serverless compute
service where you can

00:42:34.990 --> 00:42:37.040
write simple pieces of code.

00:42:37.040 --> 00:42:41.380
And in this case, it tells the
Video Intelligence API, listen,

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:43.750
there's a new video
in this bucket.

00:42:43.750 --> 00:42:47.440
Could you please process
that video with this ID?

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:51.070
It processes the video
and it stores the metadata

00:42:51.070 --> 00:42:52.630
in and out our bucket.

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:55.060
So that is what it's doing.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:57.460
Then the front end to
the demo that you see

00:42:57.460 --> 00:42:59.530
is basically looking
at both buckets.

00:42:59.530 --> 00:43:03.040
So it gets all the videos
and it extracts the metadata

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:04.820
stored in the other buckets.

00:43:04.820 --> 00:43:07.070
So that's how that works.

00:43:07.070 --> 00:43:11.000
So as I mentioned before, you
can play around with this.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:13.990
You don't have to set up
a Google Cloud Platform

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:15.940
environment if you
just want to try to see

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:17.440
how the Vision API's doing.

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:20.620
Just go to one of these
websites and play around

00:43:20.620 --> 00:43:23.870
with the demos that
are available there.

